# Experimental engine hashes served from selfhost/cdn/overlay, and the pinned
# revision each one falls back to for artifacts it did not rebuild.
#
# Format (Caddy `map` entries, one per line — imported into the CDN Caddyfile):
#   <experimentalEngineHash> <pinnedEngineHash>
#
# Written by selfhost/engine/overlay_publish.sh — you normally do not edit this
# by hand. An empty file (just these comments) means no experimental engine is
# published and the mirror behaves exactly as a passthrough cache.
# Checked in EMPTY on purpose. With no entries the mirror is a pure passthrough
# cache of Shorebird's pinned engine — which is what this repo ships. Publishing
# an experimental engine (selfhost/engine/overlay_publish.sh) writes its entry
# here; that is a local, deliberate act, not the default state.
#
# The engine device-verified on 2026-07-29 was:
#   dabf1837976819e5da33f3e143c4f628749702da 69f9831c360d9152862ec3897c67fb09ae843f3b
5b1a89658709b9a0d2e76c6f3d47ad1a26eecdee 69f9831c360d9152862ec3897c67fb09ae843f3b
760e3fabffbf31b4e86919a0ef47d6ce5f182991 69f9831c360d9152862ec3897c67fb09ae843f3b
# The Route B engine (device-verified 2026-07-30). Without an entry here a build
# against it dies on the FIRST artifact the overlay does not own — sky_engine.zip
# — because the passthrough asks upstream for OUR revision, which upstream has
# never heard of. Mapping it also switches on `must_be_local`, so a miss on an
# artifact we DO own is now a loud 404 instead of a silent fall back to stock
# bytes. sky_engine.zip is safe to take from stock: it is the Dart source of
# dart:ui, and this engine differs from the pinned one only in C++.
fc184af6509a93eaf6fc068c6820639b324175a8 69f9831c360d9152862ec3897c67fb09ae843f3b
# Our own iOS engine (selfhost/engine/publish_ios_overlay.sh). Built against
# VANILLA Dart with shorebird_use_interpreter=false, so it carries the updater and
# supports assets-only patches but not iOS code patches — those need the AOT
# linker we do not have. The hash is sha1 of the device-slice Flutter binary, so
# it changes whenever the engine does.
#
# 5a6b0b09… was the first attempt and is superseded: its host toolchain came from
# a tree still carrying Track E's killgate SDK edits, which leaked into
# platform_strong.dill and failed the iOS AOT step with "Unexpected tag 4
# (Field)". Reverting those edits changed runtime/lib/object.cc and therefore the
# VM inside Flutter.framework, hence a new hash.
#
# …and 70974f81 turned out to be fine all along. It was never the ENGINE that
# could not build an app, it was gen_snapshot: a tear-off selector applied to a
# field's implicit accessor overwrites the accessor's Field pointer. Fixed in
# engine/0004-dart-tearoff-selector-guard.patch; see HANDOFF.md for the evidence.
# Note the hash is sha1 of the Flutter binary ONLY, so a gen_snapshot-only change
# republishes under the SAME hash — clear the Flutter artifact cache
# (bin/cache/artifacts/engine/ios-release*) or a build will silently reuse the
# old compiler.
#
# 2026-08-05: republished again with patches 2 and 3a RETIRED from the compiler
# (the IsUsed(), torn_off and has_tearoff_uses gates restored to upstream), and
# with the host toolchain that makes that sound — our own dart-sdk-darwin-arm64
# plus const_finder inside darwin-arm64/artifacts.zip. Release 29.0.0+1 built,
# first frame, assets patch applied and rolled back on an iPhone 7. Engine
# binary unchanged, so the hash is the same as before. See
# selfhost/TFA_ROOT_CAUSE.md; do NOT build against this hash with a
# bin/cache/dart-sdk that is not revision 6b58bb3a.
70974f811d448da19a927c581678ef1dbd33605c 69f9831c360d9152862ec3897c67fb09ae843f3b
